.nr HY 0
.ds AD l
.TH ORGWARRIOR 1 "" "orgwarrior 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
orgwarrior \- the Org agenda from the command line, through your running Emacs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B orgwarrior
[\fIVIEW\fR]
.br
.B orgwarrior
\fIID\fR... \fIVERB\fR [\fIARGS\fR]
.br
.B orgwarrior add
[\fIKEYWORD\fR] \fItitle\fR [\fI:tag:\fR] [\fIs:DATE\fR] [\fId:DATE\fR]
.br
.B orgwarrior help
[\fITOPIC\fR | \fB\-\-man\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
A taskwarrior\-style command line for Org mode's agenda. Every task gets a short ID, and orgwarrior ID VERB acts on it. orgwarrior never parses your files: it sends each command to your running Emacs daemon with emacsclient, where Org does the work (org\-agenda, org\-todo, org\-schedule, org\-read\-date), so your agenda files, TODO keywords, custom agenda commands, logging and habits apply as in Emacs. It loads its own elisp into the daemon; nothing goes in your init file.
.PP
IDs are short hashes of a task's file and title: the same in every view and from one day to the next. Changing a keyword, tags or dates keeps the ID; renaming or refiling changes it, and the new one is printed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PD 0
.SS Views
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior
the default view (today)
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior week
this week; week +1w, week 2026\-11\-02
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior fri
one day: +2d, 2026\-10\-15
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior all
every open TODO
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior wait
by TODO state; next +@phone with a tag
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior +@phone
a tag search: +a\-b, '+a|b'
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior flu shot
a text search ('"fri"' for a date word)
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior inbox
a [views] entry from your config
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior projects.org
an agenda file's outline
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior clocked week
the time clocked
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior closed week
what was done, archived too
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior \-\-archive flu shot
a search or match of the archives too
.SS Tasks
.PP
c1 is the ID beside each task.
.sp
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1
show it
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 done
any TODO keyword; nokeyword for none
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 sched fri
due DATE too; none removes it
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 when fri 14:00
an event's date
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 +@home \-someday
tags
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 pri A
up, down, none
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 note TEXT
a note; done \-m NOTE with a state
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 refile projects
under a heading; refile alone lists them
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 up | down
among its siblings; promote, demote
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 rename NEW TITLE
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 effort 0:30
prop NAME VALUE for any property
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 clock in
clock out; clock: what's running
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 clocks
its clocks; clocks add: time already spent
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 archive | kill
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 edit | open
in Emacs, in this terminal; its links
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 c2 done
several at once
.SS Adding
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior add NEXT call mom :@phone: s:fri d:+2w
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior add event Dentist @fri 14:00\-15:00
.PD
.SH COMMANDS
.SS views
.nf
.B orgwarrior [VIEW]
.fi
.RS 2
.nf
orgwarrior             the default view: default_view in config, else today
orgwarrior today       today's agenda
orgwarrior week        this week's agenda
orgwarrior week DATE   the week of DATE: week +1w, week 2026\-11\-02
orgwarrior all         every open TODO
orgwarrior DATE        one day's agenda: fri, +2d, 2026\-10\-15 (help dates)
orgwarrior +@phone     a tag/property search, in org's syntax:
orgwarrior +a\-b        a and not b
orgwarrior '+a|b'      a or b (quote the |)
orgwarrior KEYWORD...  tasks in those TODO states (wait; done; next active)
orgwarrior next +@phone
                       a state and a tag search
orgwarrior WORDS       anything else: a text search of headings and bodies,
                       any case, part of a word too (C\-c a s)
orgwarrior '"fri"'     quoted: always a text search, for words that are
                       dates
orgwarrior todo \-SCHEDULED={.+}\-DEADLINE={.+}
                       TODOs with no date: properties match too
orgwarrior NAME        a [views] entry, NAME = "KEY": that org\-agenda custom
                       command
orgwarrior projects.org
                       an agenda file's outline: its top\-level headings,
                       done ones too, in file order (a file in a folder of
                       org\-directory: work/notes.org, or notes.org if
                       it's the only one)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Each task shows a short ID on the left; tasks commands take it.  IDs stay the same across views and over time; a renamed task gets a new one.
.PP
\-\-archive takes in the archive files too, as org's agenda does with v A:
.RS 2
.nf
orgwarrior \-\-archive flu shot      a search of everything ever archived
orgwarrior \-\-archive '+@phone'     a match; clocked \-\-archive week too
.fi
.RE
.PP
An archived task's ID shows it (orgwarrior ID); actions take only tasks in the agenda files.
.PP
A habit (org\-habit's STYLE: habit) has org's consistency graph on the line under it: a day per cell, * when it was done, ! today, colored by its state as your Emacs theme draws it (help colors).
.SS show
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID...
.fi
.PP
Show the task as org opens it: where it is (file \[u203A] outline path), the heading, the planning line, the body with each drawer (LOGBOOK, PROPERTIES) folded to one line, then its notes, newest first (help notes), and its child headings with their IDs, to show or act on in turn.  Links read as Emacs shows them; orgwarrior ID open lists and opens them (help open). Several IDs show one after another.
.RS 2
.nf
orgwarrior ID \-\-drawers   the drawers unfolded, notes and all, as in the
                          file
.fi
.RE
.PP
A task in a file that isn't writable (chmod a\-w: say a calendar a script exports to org, which the next export would overwrite) is read only, and its location line says so: shown everywhere, every change refused, never a place to refile or add to.
.SS keywords
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID... KEYWORD [\-m NOTE]
.fi
.PP
Set a TODO keyword, any of yours (the task's file's, if it has a #+TODO line), in any case: orgwarrior c1 done, orgwarrior c1 7f next.  Any heading takes one, a plain heading or an event too; nokeyword takes it off again (C\-c C\-t SPC), leaving a plain heading.
.PP
Logging follows your Emacs settings; where org would ask for a note, orgwarrior asks (orgwarrior help notes).  Repeating tasks repeat as in Emacs.
.SS dates
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID... sched DATE
.B orgwarrior ID... due DATE
.fi
.PP
DATE is anything org\-read\-date reads:
.RS 2
.nf
fri, mon          the next such day
+2d, +1w, +3m     from today
today, tomorrow
2026\-10\-15
15                the next 15th
fri 14:00         with a time
fri 14:00\-15:30   a range (or fri 14:00+1:30)
none              remove it
.fi
.RE
.PP
After the date, a repeater and a warning period, as org writes them:
.RS 2
.nf
fri .+1w          repeat (+1w, .+1w, ++1w)
2026\-11\-01 \-3d    warn 3 days before
.fi
.RE
.PP
Alone, +1w is a date (a week from today).  A new date keeps the repeater already there, as in Emacs; a whole timestamp is taken as written, so sched "<2026\-10\-16 14:00>" drops it.
.PP
orgwarrior ID... when DATE
.PP
An event's date: its plain timestamp, alone on the line under the heading (after any planning line and drawers), where add event writes it.  Changed there, or added there if it has none; none removes it.  A timestamp further down is the text's, left alone.  The same DATE as sched, a repeater too (a warning means nothing on a plain timestamp).
.PP
The same dates work for views (orgwarrior fri) and add (s:fri d:+2w).
.SS tags
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID... +TAG \-TAG
.B orgwarrior ID... tag +TAG \-TAG
.fi
.PP
Add and remove tags: orgwarrior c1 +@phone \-someday.  A tag from a #+tags: { } group replaces the others in it, as C\-c C\-q does: +@home drops @work.
.PP
After an ID, +/\- change tags; first on the line, they search (orgwarrior +@phone; help views). Tags from the file's #+filetags belong to the file: refile the task to change them.
.SS priority
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID... pri A
.B orgwarrior ID... pri up
.B orgwarrior ID... pri none
.fi
.PP
Set the priority cookie ([#A]) with org\-priority, so the range is yours: org\-priority\-highest to org\-priority\-lowest, letters (A B C by default) or numbers.  up and down step it as S\-up and S\-down do in org, which removes it past either end.  none removes it.
.SS rename
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID rename NEW TITLE
.fi
.PP
Change the title; the keyword, priority, tags and [1/3] cookies stay. Everything after rename is the title, no quotes needed (but quote shell characters).  The ID changes with the title, so the new one is printed:
.RS 2
.nf
6a \[u2192] 91  TODO call mom about the trip
.fi
.RE
.PP
A title can't start with a keyword or end in :tags: (org would read them as such): use those verbs.
.SS refile
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID... refile [TARGET]
.fi
.PP
Move the tasks under a heading from your org\-refile\-targets, as C\-c C\-w does, or to the top level of a file it names (TARGET projects.org: up to level 1). TARGET is the heading's name, or any unique part of it or of its "file \[u203A] path"; a heading wins over a file of the same name.  With no TARGET, the targets are listed.
.PP
The ID changes with the file, so the new one is printed:
.RS 2
.nf
6a \[u2192] 91  TODO call mom  \[u2192] tasks.org \[u203A] Tasks
.fi
.RE
.PP
A task already there is left alone; the others move.  Set keywords, dates and tags first, while the ID on screen still works.
.RS 2
.nf
orgwarrior ID... refile \-\-new TITLE [WHERE]
.fi
.RE
.PP
makes a plain heading TITLE first (a project, say), as the last child of WHERE, and refiles into it.  WHERE is a file from your org\-refile\-targets (the heading goes at its top level) or one of the targets; with none, config's [refile] new_in, and without that the places are listed. [refile] new_suffix goes after the title (" [/]": a count of its tasks).
.PP
A shell function makes a shortcut that keeps the IDs first:
.RS 2
.nf
owr() { orgwarrior "$@" refile tasks; }
.fi
.RE
.SS up
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID up
.B orgwarrior ID down
.fi
.PP
Move the task one place up or down among its siblings (the headings at its level under the same parent), with its subtree, as M\-up and M\-down do in Emacs.  It stays under the same parent: to move it elsewhere, refile.
.PP
orgwarrior ID demote orgwarrior ID promote
.PP
Demote puts the task, with its subtree, under the heading just above it at its level, as its last child (M\-S\-right).  Promote takes it out of its parent, to right after it.  Org's M\-S\-left promotes in place, so the headings after it at its level would become its children; promote moves it below them first, so only the task and its subtree move.
.SS archive
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID... archive
.B orgwarrior archive ID...
.fi
.PP
Archive the entries, each with its subtree, as C\-c C\-x C\-s does: to org\-archive\-location (or an ARCHIVE property), by default FILE.org_archive. Archived tasks leave the agenda, so their IDs stop working; orgwarrior \-\-archive WORDS finds them again, and the ID it shows reads one (nothing changes it).  To bring one back, open the archive file in Emacs.
.PP
A shortcut: alias owar='orgwarrior archive'
.SS kill
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID... kill [\-\-yes]
.B orgwarrior kill ID... [\-\-yes]
.fi
.PP
Delete the entries, each with its subtree, like C\-k in the agenda.  There's no undo, so it shows what goes and asks first; \-\-yes skips the question, and is needed when not at a terminal.
.PP
For "not doing this", use a done keyword (skip, if you have one): kill is for junk.
.SS edit
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID edit
.B orgwarrior edit ID
.fi
.PP
Open the task in Emacs, in this terminal: a frame of the daemon (emacsclient \-t) narrowed to the task and its subtree.  Edit the body, tick checkboxes (C\-c C\-c), anything.
.RS 2
.nf
C\-x C\-s    save
C\-x C\-c    close (asks to save first)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Changes you don't save are discarded.
.SS open
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID open [N]
.B orgwarrior open ID [N]
.fi
.PP
Open a link in the task (heading or body) in your browser: the only one at once, else pick from the list, or give its number:
.RS 2
.nf
[1] the order  https://shop.example/123
[2] https://tracking.example/abc
.fi
.RE
.PP
Over SSH (or with no graphical session) the list is printed instead, nothing opened: most terminals make the URLs clickable.  file: and other Emacs\-only links are listed; orgwarrior ID edit opens them in Emacs.  A shortcut: alias owo='orgwarrior open'
.SS clock
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID clock in [TIME]
.B orgwarrior [ID] clock out [TIME]
.B orgwarrior clock [\-\-short]
.fi
.PP
Clock in on a task, as I does in org's agenda: org writes the CLOCK line (where org\-clock\-into\-drawer says) and closes any clock running.  clock shows what's running and since when; \-\-short is one line for a status bar, nothing when no clock runs (tmux: #(orgwarrior clock \-\-short)).
.PP
TIME, when a start or stop was forgotten: a time of day, how long ago, or a date and a time.  Without one, now.  A clock out may be later than now (+10m), a clock in not: it would run before it began.
.RS 2
.nf
orgwarrior k3 clock in 9:40          orgwarrior clock out 17:30
orgwarrior k3 clock in \-15m          orgwarrior clock out +10m
orgwarrior clock out yesterday 5:30pm
.fi
.RE
.PP
What's running is the open CLOCK line in your files, not Emacs's memory: a restart loses nothing, and a clock started on another machine shows once your files sync.  One running past 4 hours is flagged "still on it?".  Two running (two machines): clock lists both, ID clock out closes one. orgwarrior [ID] clock cancel removes a clock started by mistake.
.PP
orgwarrior ID clocks orgwarrior ID clocks N START\-END | N START\- | N \-END | N rm orgwarrior ID clocks add START\-END | START DURATION
.PP
The task's clocks, newest first, numbered, then a change to one: its start, its end or both, or rm.  A time of day is on that clock's own date, an end before the start (overnight) the next day's; a date first goes with both.  Org recomputes the => total, as after S\-up on a CLOCK line in Emacs.  add logs time already spent, a running clock left alone (clock in at a past time would close it then).
.RS 2
.nf
orgwarrior k3 clocks 2 9:30\-12:20
orgwarrior k3 clocks 1 \-17:45
orgwarrior k3 clocks 2 2026\-10\-19 9:30\-12:20
orgwarrior k3 clocks add yesterday 14:00\-15:30
orgwarrior k3 clocks add 9:00 1h30m
.fi
.RE
.SS clocked
.nf
.B orgwarrior clocked [SPAN] [MATCH]
.fi
.PP
The time clocked in SPAN, as org's clocktable (C\-c C\-x C\-r) counts it, over the agenda files: each file, its headings by depth, the time each holds, and the clocked time against an Effort where there is one.
.RS 2
.nf
orgwarrior clocked                 today
orgwarrior clocked week            this week; lastweek too
orgwarrior clocked month           this month; lastmonth, year
orgwarrior clocked 2026\-10\-20      a day; 2026\-W43 a week, 2026\-10 a month
orgwarrior clocked 2026\-10\-01..2026\-10\-15
orgwarrior clocked week +@phone    a tag match after the span
.fi
.RE
.PP
A [views] entry or a page's tab can be one: clocked week.  \-\-archive counts the archive files too.
.SS closed
.nf
.B orgwarrior closed [SPAN]
.fi
.PP
What was done in SPAN: each task closed with its time (its CLOSED stamp, set by a done state), day by day, as org's agenda shows it in log mode (C\-u C\-u l).  The archive files are always included: what's done is mostly archived.  SPAN as clocked takes it:
.RS 2
.nf
orgwarrior closed                  today
orgwarrior closed week             this week; lastweek too
orgwarrior closed month            this month; lastmonth, year
orgwarrior closed 2026\-10\-20       a day; 2026\-W43 a week, 2026\-10 a month
orgwarrior closed 2026\-10\-01..2026\-10\-15
.fi
.RE
.PP
A repeating task marked done gets no CLOSED stamp (org reschedules it), so it isn't here.  A [views] entry or a page's tab can be one: closed week.
.SS properties
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID... prop NAME VALUE
.B orgwarrior ID... prop NAME none
.B orgwarrior ID... effort DURATION | none
.fi
.PP
Set a property in the task's drawer, as C\-c C\-x p does, or remove it (none).  The value is everything after the name:
.RS 2
.nf
orgwarrior k3 prop LOCATION the bank
orgwarrior k3 prop STYLE habit
.fi
.RE
.PP
Org's own (TODO, SCHEDULED, TAGS...) have their verbs instead.
.PP
Effort is the estimate, as C\-c C\-x e sets it: a duration org reads (0:30, 1:15, 2h), one of Effort_ALL's where that's set.  Clocked time shows against it.  orgwarrior ID shows the drawer.
.SS add
.nf
.B orgwarrior add [KEYWORD] title :tag: s:DATE d:DATE
.fi
.RS 2
.nf
orgwarrior add call the dentist
orgwarrior add NEXT call dentist :@phone: s:fri d:+2w
.fi
.RE
.PP
KEYWORD defaults to the target file's first open keyword (TODO, as org comes).  :a:b: adds tags; s: and d: set SCHEDULED and DEADLINE (orgwarrior help dates).  Lines after the first are the body: orgwarrior add "$(printf 'pack :travel:\en\- [ ] socks\en\- [ ] charger')". A heading there (* book flights) becomes a child, its levels kept.  A time, range, repeater or warning right after one goes with it: s:fri 14:00\-15:00 +1w.  They may go anywhere.  Quote a title with shell characters in it: orgwarrior add "why?"
.PP
Where it goes is [add] in config (orgwarrior help config); by default, the end of org\-default\-notes\-file.  \-\-to PLACE puts it elsewhere: under a heading from your refile targets, or at a file's top level, named as refile names them; \-\-under ID under any heading, by its ID:
.RS 2
.nf
orgwarrior add buy paint \-\-to reno
orgwarrior add order tiles \-\-under 4d
.fi
.RE
.PP
orgwarrior add event title @DATE [:tags:]
.RS 2
.nf
orgwarrior add event Dentist @fri 14:00\-15:00
orgwarrior add event Mom's birthday @2026\-11\-03 +1y
.fi
.RE
.PP
An event: no keyword, the date as a plain timestamp.  @DATE takes what sched does, ranges and repeaters too.  It goes to [add.event] in config, else where tasks go.  orgwarrior ID when DATE changes it after (help dates).
.SS notes
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID... note TEXT
.B orgwarrior ID... done [\-m NOTE]
.fi
.PP
A note on its own, whenever: org's "Note taken on", where org\-log\-into\-drawer puts it.  Everything after note is the text, no quotes needed; with none, it asks on the terminal.  Several IDs get the same note.
.PP
Where Emacs would ask for a note (@ in org\-todo\-keywords, org\-log\-done set to note, a LOGGING property), orgwarrior asks on the terminal:
.RS 2
.nf
Note (blank for none):
.fi
.RE
.PP
Blank logs just the timestamp, Ctrl\-C cancels and changes nothing, and \-m NOTE answers up front.  Not at a terminal: no question, no note.  The note goes through org, so org\-after\-note\-stored\-hook runs as in Emacs.
.SS config
.nf
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/orgwarrior/config.toml (default ~/.config/orgwarrior/, or
.B ORGWARRIOR_CONFIG).  All optional:
.fi
.RS 2
.nf
default_view = "today"   view for bare orgwarrior
socket = "server"        emacsclient socket
prefix_format = "..."    org\-agenda\-prefix\-format for orgwarrior only
tags = "inline"          or "right", as in Emacs
time_grid = false        org's agenda time grid
color = "auto"           or "always", "never"
.fi
.RE
.RS 2
.nf
[views]                  name = "agenda key"
dash = "d"
.fi
.RE
.RS 2
.nf
[add]                    where orgwarrior add writes
file = "~/org/inbox.org" default: org\-default\-notes\-file
heading = "Tasks"        file under it (default: top level)
captured = true          add a :CAPTURED: time
.fi
.RE
.RS 2
.nf
[add.event]              where add event writes: file, heading and captured
file = "~/org/calendar.org"
heading = "Events"       as for [add]; default: [add]'s file
.fi
.RE
.RS 2
.nf
[refile]                 refile \-\-new, and a front end's first choice
new_in = "projects.org"  where refile \-\-new makes its heading
new_suffix = " [/]"      after its title
usual = "tasks"          offered first, as refile matches it
.fi
.RE
.RS 2
.nf
[colors]                 orgwarrior help colors
.fi
.RE
.RS 2
.nf
[web]                    orgwarrior\-web's (orgwarrior itself ignores it):
name = "Sam"             whose list; [web.tabs] its tab row, label = query;
                         [web.calendar] its calendar feed
.fi
.RE
.SS colors
.nf
.B [colors] in config maps a role or TODO keyword to words from: bold dim italic
.B underline, black red green yellow blue magenta cyan white, bright\-red and the
.B like, #rrggbb (24\-bit), on\-green or on\-#rrggbb for a background, or "none".
.fi
.RS 2
.nf
NEXT = "bold cyan"
id = "blue"
todo = "bold #ff8f88"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Plain colors follow your terminal's theme.  Color is off when piped, with NO_COLOR set, or with color = "never".
.PP
Roles:
.RS 2
.nf
id header date today time extra grid now overdue deadline upcoming done\-item
priority tag todo done heading label drawer timestamp link checkbox
habit\-clear habit\-clear\-future habit\-ready habit\-ready\-future habit\-alert
habit\-alert\-future habit\-overdue habit\-overdue\-future
.fi
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-m " NOTE"
The note, for done, wait and the other keywords (help notes).
.TP
.BI \-s " SOCKET"
The Emacs server socket's name, if not the default (also socket in the config, or ORGWARRIOR_SOCKET).
.TP
.B \-\-help
This help, or after a command its own: orgwarrior add \-\-help.
.TP
.B \-\-version
The version.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/orgwarrior/config.toml
The config, all of it optional (default ~/.config/orgwarrior/config.toml); see the config topic above.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ORGWARRIOR_CONFIG
The config file, instead of the default.
.TP
.B ORGWARRIOR_SOCKET
The Emacs server socket's name.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
No color, as color = "never" does.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 when Emacs or Org refuses (an unknown ID, a bad date), or no daemon answers; 2 for a command line orgwarrior can't read.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR emacs (1),
.BR emacsclient (1);
orgwarrior-web, the same agenda as a web page: https://github.com/aleksozolins/orgwarrior\-web
.SH BUGS
Reported at https://github.com/aleksozolins/orgwarrior/issues.
